  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "ujjain",
      "phase": "rise",
      "title": "Holding back the Arabs",
      "native": "उज्जयिनी",
      "when": "c. 725 to 740 CE",
      "at": 4,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          72.4,
          24.5
        ],
        "width": 14
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "sindh",
          "to": "ujjain",
          "traveller": "arab-army",
          "style": "arab",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "After the Arab conquest of Sindh in 712, its Umayyad governors raided east, and the Arab historian al-Baladhuri says they reached Ujjain. A later Pratihara inscription at Gwalior praises Nagabhata I, the dynasty’s founder, for crushing the armies of a foreign (mlechchha) king. Further south, a Chalukya prince also claimed to have beaten the raiders, in 739.",
      "today": "Ujjain, Madhya Pradesh; where the first Pratiharas ruled from is disputed."
    },
    {
      "place": "jalore",
      "phase": "rise",
      "title": "Vatsaraja’s gamble",
      "native": "जालोर",
      "when": "778 to c. 790 CE",
      "at": 15,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76,
          25.2
        ],
        "width": 16
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "In 778 the Jain monk Udyotana finished his Prakrit novel, the Kuvalayamala, at Jabalipura (Jalore) ‘while Vatsaraja ruled’. Vatsaraja marched into the Ganga plain and beat the Pala king Dharmapala in the race for Kannauj. Then the Rashtrakuta Dhruva came north and defeated him, and Rashtrakuta grants boast that Vatsaraja fled into the desert.",
      "today": "Jalore, Rajasthan; its hill fort was rebuilt many times after the Pratiharas."
    },
    {
      "place": "kannauj",
      "phase": "rise",
      "title": "Kannauj won",
      "native": "कन्नौज",
      "when": "c. 815 CE",
      "at": 26,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78,
          26
        ],
        "width": 16
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "jalore",
          "to": "kannauj",
          "traveller": "pratihara-army",
          "style": "pratihara",
          "bend": 0.12
        }
      ],
      "text": "Vatsaraja’s son Nagabhata II was also beaten, by Govinda III, but when the Rashtrakuta went home he struck. He drove the Pala ally Chakrayudha out of Kannauj, defeated Dharmapala in the east, and made Kannauj, Harsha’s old capital, his own. For the next two centuries the Pratiharas ruled from it, and their rivals kept coming back for it.",
      "today": "Kannauj, Uttar Pradesh; the city is now best known for its perfume (attar)."
    },
    {
      "place": "gwalior",
      "phase": "height",
      "title": "A zero at Gwalior",
      "native": "ग्वालियर",
      "when": "876 CE",
      "at": 38,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.5,
          26.3
        ],
        "width": 20
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Mihira Bhoja, Nagabhata’s grandson, ruled the north for about fifty years. In 876, under him, a small rock-cut temple to Vishnu at Gwalior fort was given land, and its inscription writes the numbers 270 and 50 with a round zero. It is one of the oldest dated zeros written in India, and it can still be read there.",
      "today": "The Chaturbhuj temple, Gwalior Fort, Madhya Pradesh."
    },
    {
      "place": "kannauj",
      "phase": "height",
      "title": "Poets at Mahodaya",
      "native": "महोदय",
      "when": "c. 885 to 910 CE",
      "at": 48,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.5,
          26.3
        ],
        "width": 20
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Under Bhoja’s son Mahendrapala I, Kannauj, which court poets called Mahodaya, was the richest city of the north. Mahendrapala’s teacher Rajashekhara wrote plays in Sanskrit and Prakrit and the Kavyamimamsa, a handbook for poets that quotes his wife Avantisundari as a critic. The empire then stretched from Saurashtra and the edge of Sindh to north Bengal.",
      "today": "Kannauj, Uttar Pradesh; Rajashekhara’s Karpuramanjari is still studied as a Prakrit classic."
    },
    {
      "place": "kannauj",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "The Rashtrakutas sack Kannauj",
      "native": "कन्नौज",
      "when": "c. 916 CE",
      "at": 60,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78,
          22
        ],
        "width": 20
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "manyakheta",
          "to": "kannauj",
          "traveller": "rashtrakuta-army",
          "style": "rashtrakuta",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "In about 916 the Rashtrakuta king Indra III marched north and sacked Kannauj, and the Pratihara Mahipala fled. The Kannada poet Pampa says Indra’s feudatory bathed his horses where the Ganga meets the Yamuna. Mahipala recovered his capital, but the empire never fully recovered its strength. The Arab traveller al-Masudi, in India about then, described its armies.",
      "today": "Prayagraj, where the Ganga and the Yamuna meet."
    },
    {
      "place": "khajuraho",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "The Chandelas break away",
      "native": "खजुराहो",
      "when": "c. 954 CE",
      "at": 72,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          79.6,
          25.2
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Pratihara governors and vassals now built kingdoms of their own. The Chandelas of Khajuraho had served the Pratiharas, but an inscription of 954 says Yashovarman had taken an image of Vishnu from the Pratihara king and set it in his new temple, the Lakshmana. His son Dhanga ruled as an independent king. Khajuraho’s temples rose under them.",
      "today": "The Khajuraho temples, Madhya Pradesh, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1986."
    },
    {
      "place": "kannauj",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "Mahmud comes to Kannauj",
      "native": "कन्नौज",
      "when": "1018 CE",
      "at": 84,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76,
          30.3
        ],
        "width": 20
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "ghazni",
          "to": "kannauj",
          "traveller": "mahmud-army",
          "style": "ghaznavid",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "In 1018 Mahmud of Ghazni raided into the Ganga plain. The Pratihara king Rajyapala abandoned Kannauj, and Mahmud’s army plundered its temples. Neighbouring kings were outraged at his flight: an inscription says a vassal of the Chandela Vidyadhara killed Rajyapala. Pratiharas lingered for a few more years, but the empire that had ruled the north was gone.",
      "today": "Ghazni, Afghanistan; Mahmud’s tomb is at Rauza, just outside the town."
    },
    {
      "place": "varanasi",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "After the Pratiharas",
      "native": "वाराणसी",
      "when": "c. 1036 to 1090 CE",
      "at": 95,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          81.5,
          26
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The last Pratiharas known from inscriptions, Trilochanapala and Yashahpala, held only a small kingdom around Kannauj into the 1030s. In about 1090 a new dynasty, the Gahadavalas, took Kannauj and ruled from it and from Varanasi. They held the city until 1194, when Muhammad of Ghor defeated Jayachandra, opening the way to the Delhi Sultanate.",
      "today": "Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh, the Gahadavalas’ second capital."
    }
  ],
