  "people": {
    "heading": "Kings and poets",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "vatsaraja",
        "name": "Vatsaraja",
        "native": "वत्सराज",
        "life": "r. c. 775 to 805",
        "role": "Pratihara king who opened the struggle for Kannauj.",
        "steps": [
          1
        ],
        "text": "Ruling in Rajasthan and Malwa, he was the first Pratihara to strike into the Ganga plain, beating Dharmapala of Bengal. The Rashtrakuta Dhruva then defeated him, and he had to give up his gains. Jain writers of his time, Udyotana and Jinasena, name him in their works, which is how his dates are known."
      },
      {
        "id": "mihira-bhoja",
        "name": "Mihira Bhoja",
        "native": "मिहिर भोज",
        "life": "r. c. 836 to 885",
        "role": "The most powerful Pratihara king.",
        "steps": [
          3,
          4
        ],
        "text": "He called himself Adivaraha, after Vishnu’s boar form, and put a boar on his coins. In about 851 the Arab merchant Sulaiman wrote that the Gurjara king had the finest cavalry in India and was a great enemy of the Arabs, and that his land was safe from robbers. He fought the Palas and Rashtrakutas throughout his reign."
      },
      {
        "id": "rajashekhara",
        "name": "Rajashekhara",
        "native": "राजशेखर",
        "life": "fl. c. 880 to 920",
        "role": "Poet and critic at the Pratihara court.",
        "steps": [
          4,
          5
        ],
        "text": "Teacher of Mahendrapala I and poet of his son Mahipala, he came from a family of poets in the Deccan. He wrote the Karpuramanjari, a play entirely in Prakrit, a Sanskrit Ramayana play and the Kavyamimamsa, a guide to the poet’s craft. He quotes his wife Avantisundari, one of the few women critics of the time whose views survive."
      }
    ]
  }
